            <div
                class="p-8 md:p-12 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed max-w-4xl"
                itemprop="articleBody"
            >
                <p class="text-lg mb-10 text-slate-400">
                    Every veteran hobbyist knows the "box-art betrayal." You buy
                    a box of "28mm" infantry to reinforce your platoon, but once
                    they're on the bench, they look like middle-schoolers
                    standing next to your existing squad. One manufacturer's
                    "28mm" is another's "32mm," and the industry-standard ruler
                    seems to be made of rubber.
                </p>
                <p class="mb-10">
                    This isn't just poor quality control; it's the result of <strong
                        class="text-white">"Scale Creep,"</strong
                    > a decades-long evolution where miniatures have slowly put on
                    volumetric bulk. As a technical consultant in this hobby, I've
                    seen players tear their hair out trying to maintain a cohesive
                    tabletop. The truth is, "scale" in wargaming is less a mathematical
                    constant and more of a stylistic suggestion. To build a truly
                    uniform army, you have to look past the label and understand
                    the mechanics of the lie.
                </p>

                <!-- Point 1 -->
                <div id="point-1" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-4">
                        <span
                            class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-[#ff6b35]/10 text-[#ff6b35] text-sm font-black flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
                            >1</span
                        >
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading text-white">
                            The 28mm Mystery: Reference Points and the 1:60 Trap
                        </h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pl-14">
                        <p class="mb-4">
                            The fundamental discrepancy in height starts with
                            where the manufacturer places their ruler. In the
                            history of the hobby, the "Standard-gauge" for a
                            175cm-180cm human was often mapped to a <strong
                                class="text-white">1:60 ratio</strong
                            >—the classic "1 inch = 5 feet" rule used in
                            countless battlemaps. Mathematically, a 1:60 human
                            should be 30mm tall. So why are they called 28mm?
                        </p>
                        <p class="mb-6">
                            The answer lies in the <strong class="text-white"
                                >"chosen reference point."</strong
                            > As the expert source at Alkony notes, manufacturers
                            are rarely transparent about where they stop measuring.
                        </p>
                        <blockquote
                            class="border-l-4 border-[#ff6b35] pl-6 py-2 my-6 bg-[#ff6b35]/5 rounded-r-xl"
                        >
                            <p class="text-slate-300 italic mb-2">
                                "A 28mm miniature means that the size of the
                                miniature will be 28mm from the feet of the mini
                                to the chosen reference point."
                            </p>
                            <cite
                                class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-[#ff6b35] not-italic"
                                >— Alkony</cite
                            >
                        </blockquote>
                        <p class="mb-4">
                            In most cases, that reference point is the <strong
                                class="text-white">eye level</strong
                            >, not the top of the head. This is a practical
                            concession; it's easier for a sculptor to find the
                            eyes than the crown of a head obscured by a helmet
                            or a mitre cap. However, by the time you add the
                            forehead and headgear, a "28mm" model actually
                            stands 30mm to 32mm tall.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            This creates a <strong class="text-white"
                                >"marketing vs. reality"</strong
                            > conflict: you are often buying 32mm models that have
                            simply been grandfathered into a 28mm label for the sake
                            of branding.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Point 2 -->
                <div id="point-2" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-4">
                        <span
                            class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-[#ff6b35]/10 text-[#ff6b35] text-sm font-black flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
                            >2</span
                        >
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading text-white">
                            The Bolt Action Secret: Why 1:48 Tanks Often Look
                            Better Than 1:56
                        </h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pl-14">
                        <p class="mb-4">
                            In Bolt Action, the "official" vehicle scale is
                            1:56. On paper, this is the correct ratio for 28mm.
                            However, walk into any tournament and you'll see <strong
                                class="text-white"
                                >Tamiya 1:48 scale kits</strong
                            > dominating the motor pool. This isn't an accident.
                        </p>
                        <p class="mb-6">
                            The issue is Warlord Games' internal inconsistency.
                            Their metal miniatures are generally traditional
                            28mm (closer to a 1:52 or 1:56 ratio), but their
                            newer plastic sprues are actually <strong
                                class="text-white">32mm</strong
                            >. Because these plastics are "beefier" and taller,
                            a true 1:56 tank looks like a toy next to them. The
                            "volumetric bulk" of the infantry makes the vehicle
                            appear as though the crew couldn't possibly fit
                            inside.
                        </p>
                        <blockquote
                            class="border-l-4 border-[#ff6b35] pl-6 py-2 my-6 bg-[#ff6b35]/5 rounded-r-xl"
                        >
                            <p class="text-slate-300 italic mb-2">
                                "I believe they fit with the oversized nature of
                                Warlord's infantry better than the actual
                                Warlord tanks themselves."
                            </p>
                            <cite
                                class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-[#ff6b35] not-italic"
                                >— Reddit (r/boltaction)</cite
                            >
                        </blockquote>
                        <p class="mb-4">
                            Beyond the visual immersion, there's a cold, hard
                            fiscal reality: 1:48 Tamiya kits are widely
                            available at hobby shops and often cost <strong
                                class="text-white">half the price</strong
                            > of "official" wargaming-branded 1:56 kits.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            For the technical hobbyist, 1:48 provides a rare
                            win-win: better visual proportions and a
                            significantly lower cost of entry. Use our <a rel="nofollow"
                                href="/?source=1:48&target=32mm&logic=eye"
                                class="text-[#ff6b35] hover:text-white transition-colors font-semibold"
                                >1:48 to 32mm conversion</a
                            > in the Scale Engine to verify your own builds.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Point 3 -->
                <div id="point-3" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-4">
                        <span
                            class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-[#ff6b35]/10 text-[#ff6b35] text-sm font-black flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
                            >3</span
                        >
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading text-white">
                            The "Stay Puft" Effect: Why Hands and Heads are
                            Growing
                        </h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pl-14">
                        <p class="mb-6">
                            <strong class="text-white">"Heroic Scale"</strong> is
                            often dismissed as a lack of anatomical skill, but it
                            is actually a functional design choice. From three feet
                            away—the standard "tabletop distance"—a realistically
                            proportioned 28mm head is roughly the size of a peppercorn.
                        </p>
                        <blockquote
                            class="border-l-4 border-[#ff6b35] pl-6 py-2 my-6 bg-[#ff6b35]/5 rounded-r-xl"
                        >
                            <p class="text-slate-300 italic mb-2">
                                "From a Tabletop distance, a realistically
                                scaled figure will have unintelligible hands and
                                heads, and if they are scaled up to be readable,
                                the feet look tiny."
                            </p>
                            <cite
                                class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-[#ff6b35] not-italic"
                                >— Zandoria Studios</cite
                            >
                        </blockquote>
                        <p class="mb-4">
                            To make models "readable" and easier to paint,
                            sculptors exaggerate the heads, hands, and weapons.
                            However, this has led to a divisive phenomenon known
                            in the Warlord Games forums as the <strong
                                class="text-white"
                                >"Stay Puft Marshmallow Man" effect</strong
                            >.
                        </p>
                        <p class="mb-4">
                            You can see this clearly in the brand-new plastic
                            German Veteran and US Airborne kits. They are so
                            "puffy-beefy" that they are functionally
                            incompatible with older "True Scale" kits.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            This makes <strong class="text-white"
                                >"kit-bashing"</strong
                            >—mixing parts from different sets—an exercise in
                            frustration, as a new-style arm on an old-style body
                            looks like a prosthetic from a different species.
                            Before you commit to a conversion, cross-reference
                            the source scales in our <a
                                href="#detailedTable"
                                class="text-[#ff6b35] hover:text-white transition-colors font-semibold"
                                >Dual Methodology table</a
                            > above.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Point 4 -->
                <div id="point-4" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-4">
                        <span
                            class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-[#ff6b35]/10 text-[#ff6b35] text-sm font-black flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
                            >4</span
                        >
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading text-white">
                            The Silent Scale-Killer: The 5mm Base Factor
                        </h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pl-14">
                        <p class="mb-4">
                            The most overlooked factor in tabletop scale isn't
                            the model; it's the <strong class="text-white"
                                >plastic disc it stands on</strong
                            >. Infantry are almost always based on 2mm to 5mm
                            thick plastic, while vehicles—especially in
                            historical games—are frequently left unbased.
                        </p>
                        <p class="mb-6">
                            This creates a <strong class="text-white"
                                >3-5mm "false height"</strong
                            > disparity. When you place a 32mm soldier on a 4mm base,
                            his eye level is now 36mm above the table. If he stands
                            next to a 1:56 scale Tiger tank resting directly on the
                            mat, he will tower over the engine deck.
                        </p>
                        <p class="mb-4">
                            This gap in height is the primary reason why
                            "official" scales feel wrong; the base effectively
                            "raises" the infantry's scale while the vehicle
                            remains grounded.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            This is where 3D printing has become the <strong
                                class="text-white">"Great Equalizer."</strong
                            > Technical hobbyists no longer have to accept the manufacturer's
                            fixed height; they can use digital scaling to compensate
                            for base thickness and ensure that the infantry and armor
                            share the same visual horizon. Our <a
                                href="/"
                                class="text-[#ff6b35] hover:text-white transition-colors font-semibold"
                                >Scale Engine</a
                            > accounts for both Eye Level and Top of Head methodologies
                            to help you dial this in precisely.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Point 5 -->
                <div id="point-5" class="mb-14 scroll-mt-28">
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-4">
                        <span
                            class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-[#ff6b35]/10 text-[#ff6b35] text-sm font-black flex items-center justify-center shrink-0"
                            >5</span
                        >
                        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold font-heading text-white">
                            The 3D Printing Math: Precision Sizing for the
                            Workbench
                        </h3>
                    </div>
                    <div class="pl-14">
                        <p class="mb-6">
                            Digital manufacturing has shifted the power from the
                            factory to the player. By using a universal <strong
                                class="text-white">175cm human baseline</strong
                            >, we can finally bridge the gap between "28mm"
                            (D&D/Historical) and "32mm" (Warhammer/Modern)
                            systems. If you are mixing STL files from different
                            designers, you need to account for both the
                            measurement methodology and material physics.
